<%# The connector's documentation. #359.

    /docs returned 404, and both directory submissions require a public documentation URL
    before they will accept anything, so this blocks the listing tickets as much as the tool
    annotations do. #342 found it; assets/robots.txt predicted it in its own closing comment.

    **Written for a lifter, not a maintainer.** It is mostly the second half of README.md said
    again for somebody who wants to use the thing rather than run it: how to add it, what it
    can do, what it cannot, and what is stored. The directory forms ask about data handling
    too, so writing that honestly once answers the form as well as the reader.

    The one-click link leads, because until a directory listing exists it is the entire
    distribution mechanism: it opens Claude's add-connector dialog prefilled, needs no listing
    and no review, and works for a signed-out reader -- they sign in and land back on the
    dialog.

    Prose rather than a table of tools. A generated list of thirty-odd tool names is a thing
    this page could produce automatically and nobody would read; what a reader needs is
    whether the connector can do the thing they are about to ask for, which is four sentences.
    It would also go stale in the opposite direction from the truth -- a list that is right
    about names and silent about what they mean. %>
<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
  <h1 class="serif text-4xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Connect tectonic plates to Claude or ChatGPT</h1>

  <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-gray-700">
    tectonic plates has a connector, so you can plan and log training by asking for it.
    Write a whole block in conversation, have it land in the app ready to lift, and tick sets
    off on your phone at the rack.
  </p>

  <%# The link, at the top, because it is the thing this page is for. `connectorUrl` is built
      from the same two constants the app serves itself under, so a change to either cannot
      leave this pointing at an endpoint that moved. %>
  <div class="mt-8 rounded-xl border border-sky-200 bg-sky-50 p-5">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Add it in one click</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
      Opens Claude&rsquo;s add-connector dialog with the address already filled in. If you are
      not signed in to Claude it will ask you to, then bring you back here.
    </p>
    <a href="<%= claude_connector_url %>" rel="noopener"
       class="<%= button_style %> mt-4 inline-flex min-h-11 items-center bg-sky-800 px-4 text-sm
              font-semibold text-white hover:bg-sky-900">
      Add to Claude
    </a>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-gray-600">
      Adding it by hand instead, in Claude or in ChatGPT? The address is
      <code class="rounded bg-white px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs"><%= mcp_endpoint_url %></code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">What it can do</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">
    It writes training, not just records it. You can ask for a whole block &mdash; weeks, the
    days in each week, the lifts in each day, how each one progresses, which weeks deload
    &mdash; and then ask for a week of it to be written into real sessions. The warmup ramps
    are worked out for you, and every weight is rounded to something your own rack can
    actually load, from the bar and plates you list on the settings page.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">
    It reads training too: what you lifted and when, what a movement&rsquo;s history implies
    about your current max, how long you typically rest between sets, and how a block is
    going against what it asked for. Asking &ldquo;how did Monday go&rdquo; or &ldquo;what
    should bench open at next block&rdquo; is a question it can answer from your own numbers.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">
    And it logs: a set, a session, a correction to either. Sets you log in conversation and
    sets you tap off on the session screen are the same rows, so a session started one way
    can be finished the other.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">What it will not do</h2>
  <%# The honest half, and the more useful one. A reader deciding whether to connect an app to
      an assistant is deciding what it might do without being asked, and a page that only
      lists capabilities answers the wrong question. %>
  <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-gray-700">
    <li>
      It never reaches another account. Every tool acts only on the account that authorised
      it, and there is no tool that can name a different one.
    </li>
    <li>
      It will not quietly overwrite training you have already logged. Changing the weight or
      reps of a set you marked as lifted has to be confirmed, and changing which movement such
      a set was is refused outright &mdash; that is a different set, not an edited one.
    </li>
    <li>
      It does not decide anything for you. The app measures and reports; how long to rest,
      what to open a block at, and whether a session was any good are judgements, and they
      stay with you and whoever you are talking to.
    </li>
    <li>
      It cannot read your email, your files, or anything else. It reaches this app and nothing
      else, over one address.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Turning it off</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">
    Authorisation is per account and revocable at any time from
    <a href="/connections" class="text-lime-700 underline hover:text-lime-800">your connections
    page</a>. Revoking takes effect immediately: the token stops working on the next call, and
    nothing you have already logged is touched.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">What is stored</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">
    Your email address, so you can sign in, and your training: workouts, sets, the movements
    you do them on, the blocks you write, the plates you own, and your time zone. That is the
    whole of it. There is no bodyweight, no measurements, no photographs and no health data of
    any other kind.
  </p>
  <%# Written against the code rather than from memory, and corrected once while writing it: a
      first draft here said the audit log records field names and never values, which is true
      of the error reports and not of the log. McpAuditLog.record stores the whole argument
      hash. That is the right design -- an audit trail that cannot say what was written is not
      one -- and the page has to say which is which rather than claim the stricter of the two
      about both. %>
  <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">
    When an assistant writes a row, the row records that it did &mdash; so a set you can&rsquo;t
    account for says where it came from. Every write through the connector is kept in your own
    account&rsquo;s audit trail, with what was sent, so you can see exactly what was done on
    your behalf. It stays in this app&rsquo;s database.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">
    If something breaks, the crash report that goes to our error monitoring carries the names
    of the fields involved and never their values, and never your email address.
  </p>
  <%# Not "the full terms are on the about page", which the first draft of this said. #346
      wrote them and deliberately did not publish them: they sit in legal/ with no route and no
      link, because publishing is an act with legal consequence and section 6 is waiting on a
      lawyer's read. A documentation page that links to terms that are not served would be a
      404 on the one page a directory submission points at, and a page that says "the terms
      are" about a draft would be worse than that. So this says what is true today. %>
  <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">
    More about what this app is for is on the
    <a href="/about" class="text-lime-700 underline hover:text-lime-800">about page</a>.
  </p>
</div>
